## Model & Reasoning

**Model (review paths are PINNED — WGS fork):** Review, Challenge, and the
custom-instructions Review path all pin the reviewer explicitly via the
`{{CODEX_REVIEW_MODEL_FLAGS}}` token, whose single source of truth is
`scripts/resolvers/constants.ts`. Today that resolves to:

```
-c 'model="gpt-6-astra"' -c 'review_model="gpt-6-astra"' -c 'model_reasoning_effort="max"'
```

Upstream gstack hardcodes no model, so /codex inherits the `model = ` line in
`~/.codex/config.toml`. For a *reviewer* that is a bug, not a feature: the point of the
Codex pass is an independent, maximum-quality outside read, and inheriting the config
default silently downgrades it to whatever was last pinned there. The pin is explicit so
it cannot drift.

`review_model` is pinned alongside `model` deliberately. It is a real config key in
codex-cli 0.153.4, but setting it alone does NOT switch the reviewer (verified: the
session header still reported the `model` value). It is pinned defensively so a
`review_model = ` line landing in `config.toml` later cannot silently select an older
reviewer behind the skill's back.

**Consult mode is NOT pinned.** It keeps the inherited default and `medium` effort — it
is an interactive advice path where speed matters, not a reviewer.

If the user asks for a different model, their request wins — pass it through (see the
flag-shape note below).

**Reasoning effort (per-mode defaults):**
- **Review (2A):** `max` — pinned; review quality is the priority
- **Challenge (2B):** `max` — pinned; adversarial depth is the whole job
- **Consult (2C):** `medium` — large context, interactive, needs speed (unpinned)

**Why `max` and not `ultra`.** `codex debug models` lists six levels for `gpt-6-astra`:
low, medium, high, xhigh, max, ultra. `max` is "Maximum reasoning depth for the hardest
problems". `ultra` is "Maximum reasoning **with automatic task delegation**" — it is not
simply more thinking, it lets the model spawn delegated sub-tasks. That breaks the
bounded, read-only, single-shot contract every review path here depends on (one timeout
wrapper, one captured stderr, one verdict). `max` is the deepest reasoning that stays
bounded. Do not "upgrade" these pins to `ultra` without redesigning the timeout and
capture story.

**Cost and time.** `max` is far slower and more expensive than the `high` these paths
used before (`xhigh` alone is ~23x `high`; OpenAI issues #8545, #8402, #6931). That is a
deliberate trade for review quality. Every wrapper was widened to 1200s with a 1260000ms
Bash gate above it, so execution stays bounded and a stall still surfaces as a
diagnosable exit 124 rather than a silent harness kill.

The `--xhigh` flag still works as a user override and now means *less* reasoning than the
default, not more.

**Web search:** All codex commands pass `{{CODEX_WEB_SEARCH_FLAG}}` so `codex exec`
invocations can look up docs and APIs during review. This is OpenAI's cached index —
fast, no extra cost. Unlike the legacy `--enable`-based spelling (deprecated by
codex >=0.144), the `-c` form explicitly overrides any top-level
`web_search` setting in `~/.codex/config.toml`. Note: native `codex review` disables
web search regardless of configuration, so on the default Review path the flag is a
harmless no-op — only exec-based modes actually search.

If the user specifies a model (e.g., `/codex review -m gpt-5.1-codex-max` or
`/codex challenge -m gpt-5.2`), the flag to pass depends on the underlying command:

- **Exec-based modes** (Challenge, Consult, and the custom-instructions Review path)
  run `codex exec`, which takes `-m <model>` — pass it through as-is.
- **Default Review mode** runs `codex review`, which REJECTS `-m`
  (`error: unexpected argument '-m' found`, verified on 0.147.0 — its help lists no
  `-m`/`--model` option). Translate the user's `-m <model>` into the config form:
  `-c model="<model>"`. Same shape as the `--base`-vs-prompt incompatibility above:
  review mode takes its knobs through flags/config, never through extra arguments.

